  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nEarly this morning, GOES WV imagery shows broad mid level troughing \nacross central Canada which largely will not change until mid next \nweek, yielding quazi-zonal flow over the Upper Great Lakes. This \nmeans that through early next week, precip chances will be supported \nby diurnal instability and driven by transient shortwaves/frontal \nboundaries. One such shortwave is currently moving into Minnesota, \nwhile at the surface, a weak frontal boundary is sinking over \nnorthern Minnesota and Lake Superior and kicking off an initial \nround of showers. Weak elevated instability (<500 J/kg) is analyzed \nover the area with strong shear (~50 kts), so thunder can't be ruled \nout as convection continues to kick off closer to home, but strong \nto severe storms are not expected. This initial round hangs out \nmostly over Lake Superior and the Keweenaw. PoPs expand to the rest \nof the UP the remainder of the day as diurnal instability builds, \nthe front continues to sag over the CWA, and a upper level jet \nstreak passes overhead. Instability will be run of the mill for \nmeager summertime convection (mean values well below 1000 J/kg) with \nhealthy bulk shear up to 40 kts. Given lackluster moisture and \nforcing, severe storms are not expected. CAMs are not in great \nagreement on coverage and timing of showers and storms following \nthis morning's batch, reflected in an unimpressive 20-50% chances \nfor rainfall amounts exceeding 0.25 inches per ensembles. \n\nExpect an otherwise quiet and partly to mostly cloudy day with \ntemperatures rising in the 70s for most, and some isolated spots \npeaking near 80. \n\nDry weather returns for tonight and Monday as weak surface ridging \nrecovers. Lows dip into normal temps in the upper 40s to upper 50s. \nThe rest of the forecast sees a slow trend to slightly cooler than \nnormal temps as highs trend toward upper 60s to mid 70s for the end \nof the work week with lows in the 40s to mid 50s. Shortwaves Tuesday \nand Wednesday bring low chances for showers and storms (mostly 15-\n30% chance), then drier weather is favored into the weekend as \nstronger high pressure returns.",
